Let's create a program like rsync, but that can do bi-directional syncing of multiple directories at a time.  The CLI would look something like:
"xsync [SERVERNAME] [GLOBAL_ARGS] <DIRECTORY_1> [DIRECTORY_ARGS] [<DIRECTORY_2>...]".  The default operation would be "in-out", but could also be specified as "--in" or "--out" to only operate one direction.  The default would be to sync a directory to the same directory on the destination, but the user could specify "--dest" to override that.  The process would use the mtime of the file to determine which way to sync, the newer file would be used as the source.  By default it would expect to access the remote machine via SSH like rsync does, with an "--ssh" override option to allow the user to specify the SSH command to use.  The remote machine is expected to have the xsync program installed.  They will need to handshake a version so that they can know they are talking to compatible endpoints.  The end running on the "ssh" endpoint would run in a client mode.  The server would tell it the directory being operated on, any options it needs to know, and then would go into sync mode.  At the end of the sync it could then provide another directory or could send an END message.  The sync mode would need to collect a local and remote list of files/directories, recursively, including mtime for the files, and then determine which direction the sync needed to go and start requesting files and directories (a directory request would mostly be about getting ownership/attr/permissions correct), and transferring the file data using the rsync smart transfer algorithm to only transfer changed data. In the in+out mode, a file that exists but is missing on the other side will need to be treated as a transfer rather than a delete since we don't know which side is most recent.  We should have an "--exclude" option, and a robust/useful/modern "--progress" mode.

Lets make this a rust program.  This program should have robust test suite (likely stubbing out the ssh so you can run the local and remote version directly without needing ssh auth set up).

